# Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues in the current repository on the GitHub Projects board of acme, project 3, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml`. Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of the current repository; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

## Lifecycle

- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, only when it is closed.
- A blocker whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) counts as unresolved.

## Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N`; `--dry-run` prints its plan (status, links, the comment it would post) without changing anything, and `--show` also prints the issue's card, as `gh dojo show N` does. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line: there is no need to read the issue again.

### `go`

**When:** Go

`gh dojo go N`: any status → Doing.

## Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue: its status, milestone, labels, blockers both ways (unresolved first, each with its board status), its parent and sub-issues (the same way), status history, description and the last 5 comments; a line left out means none. Several numbers, `gh dojo show 12 13`, print three lines per issue: read the one you take alone. `--comments all` prints the whole thread (up to 100), `--full` everything uncut.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.

## Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <label> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board, in one command; a long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. More labels, assignees, a milestone, blockers or a parent: `gh dojo new --help`.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` / `gh dojo unblock N --by M`: add or remove a blocked-by link without changing the status. An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M`.

## Repeats are safe

Run the same command again after an error: a verb does not set a status that is already set, nor post a comment that is already the latest from gh-dojo; `comment` does not post its last comment again; `block` and `unblock` skip links that are already as asked; `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created instead of creating another.

## Exit codes

On an error, read its `error:`, `allowed:` and `fix:` lines and do what `fix:` says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later.

## JSON

Every command takes `--json`, `--jq` and `--template`, as gh does, for picking values out; for everyday work the text is shorter.
